 satisﬁed by the phase-ﬁeld whenever ¤푑> 0, 퐺푐 ℓ  푑−ℓ2Δ푑  = 2(1 −푑) ␀휓+ −휓푐푟푖푡  in Ω, ∇푑· n = 0 on 휕Ω. (2.28) As in most models, fracture is designed to be irreversible, with a requirement on thermodynamic consistency (Miehe et al. 2010b). The rate of change of Γℓ(푑) deﬁnes the dissipation of power used in creating the crack ﬁeld ¤Γℓ(푑) = ∫ Ω 퐺푐훿푑훾(푑, ∇푑) ¤푑d푉, where 훿푑훾(푑, ∇푑) is the variational derivative of the crack density function. Rapids articles must not exceed this page length 11 To ensure thermodynamically-consistent, irreversible fracture, we require that the dissi- pated power is positive ¤Γℓ(푑) ⩾0. This condition can be satisﬁed by locally imposing that 훿푑훾(푑, ∇푑) ⩾0, (2.29) ¤푑⩾0. (2.30) The second inequality is a natural constraint that assumes the phase-ﬁeld evolution is locally irreversible and does not account for any healing. In order to satisfy the conditions (2.29) and (2.30), we follow (Miehe et al. 2010b) and introduce a local history ﬁeld of maximum tensile energy over time or loading steps H(푥, 푡) := max 푠⟨휓+(휺푒(푥, 푠)) −휓푐푟푖푡⟩+. (2.31) The fracture phase-ﬁeld evolution is then modiﬁed to be 퐺푐 ℓ  푑−ℓ2Δ푑  = 2(1 −푑)H in Ω, ∇푑· n = 0 on 휕Ω. (2.32) We will use this formulation for the implementation. For completeness, we present the full system of equations derived from the modiﬁed expressions for Πint and Πext below. To simplify the dependence of these equations upon ma- terial parameters, we present them in non-dimensional form. This is obtained by introducing non-dimensional scaled quantities (labelled by asterix) deﬁned as follows: x = 퐿x∗, 푡= 휏푡∗, u = 푢푐u∗, w = 푢푐w∗, ℓ= 퐿ℓ∗, 휆= 휇푐휆∗, 휇= 휇푐휇∗, 푝푤= 푝푤푐푝∗ 푤, f = 푓푐f∗, t = 푝푐t∗, 휌푠= 휌푐휌∗ 푠, 휌푤= 휌푐휌∗ 푤, 휂= 휂푐휂∗, 흈= 푝푐흈∗, 푝= 푝푐푝∗, H = H푐H∗, 휓= H푐휓∗, 휓± = H푐휓±∗, 휓푐푟푖푡= H푐휓∗ 푐푟푖푡. (2.33) The scaling of 푥, 푦, 푧is to be understood as the scaling of all physical dimensions by a characteristic scale for the ice thickness 퐿. Similarly, time is scaled by 휏and displacements by 푢푐. The other scales are