tions associated with a range of parameter values for ℎ, 𝛼, ℓ, 𝑛, 𝑚, and 𝛾, further generalizing our results to nonlinear rheology (𝑛≠1) and sliding behavior (𝑚≠1). For the comparisons between the numerical results and our analytical model, we first discuss the relationship between the two types of basal stress parameterizations used. Namely, in the analytical model, we have used 𝜏𝑏= 𝐶𝑢𝑚(equation 2.2), which gives the shear stress in terms of the depth-averaged velocity, 𝑢. In contrast, the numerical experiments use 𝜏𝑏= 𝐶𝑏𝑢𝑚 𝑏, (2.19) where 𝐶𝑏is the basal sliding coefficient we feed to the numerical simulations. Equation 2.19 relates to the parameterization used in the analytical model (equation 2.2) via: 𝐶= 𝐶𝑏 𝑢𝑏 𝑢 𝑚 . (2.20) The ratio of sliding velocity, 𝑢𝑏to total depth-averaged velocity, 𝑢, can be written, (e.g., see equation A 1), as 𝑢𝑏 𝑢= (𝜌𝑔ℎ𝛼)1/𝑚𝐶−1/𝑚 𝑏 2𝐴 𝑛+2 (𝜌𝑔ℎ𝛼)𝑛ℎ+ (𝜌𝑔ℎ𝛼)1/𝑚𝐶−1/𝑚 𝑏 . (2.21) In order to draw comparisons between the scaling of the coupling length and the numerical experiments (e.g., §3), we must first return to the definition of 𝛾(equation 2.4), noticing that it is defined in terms of 𝐶, but 𝐶𝑏is what we have fed to the Elmer simulations. To bridge this gap, we substitute equation 2.20 into equation 2.4 to obtain 𝛾= 𝐴ℎ(𝜌𝑔ℎ𝛼)𝑛−1/𝑚𝐶1/𝑚 𝑏 𝑢𝑏 𝑢  , (2.22) where the ratio ␀ 𝑢𝑏 𝑢  is given in equation 2.21. 2.3.1. Cases of 𝑛= 1 𝑚= 1 For comparisons between the analytical model and the cases where 𝑚= 1 𝑛= 1, equation 2.21 simplifies to 𝑢𝑏 𝑢= 3 2𝐴ℎ𝐶𝑏+ 3 , (2.23) and equation 2.22 becomes 𝛾𝑎𝑛= 3𝐴ℎ𝐶𝑏 2𝐴ℎ𝐶𝑏+ 3 , (2.24) where the [∗]𝑎𝑛subscript denotes that this applies to the analytical model. To parameterize the end-member no slip (outside of the slippery patch) cases into our SSA-based analytical model, we effectively increase the value of 𝐶𝑏→∞, which eliminates basal velocity, i.e., 𝑢𝑏→0. In this case, we observe the following limit 𝛾𝑛𝑠= lim 𝐶𝑏→∞𝛾= 3 2 , (2.25) where the [∗]𝑛𝑠subscript stands for ‘no slip’ (outside of the slippery patch). This serves as the upper limit value used for the analytical model corresponding to the no slip cases in the numerical simulations. 8 3. Numerical simulations We used the finite element full-Stokes solver Elmer/Ice to conduct a suite of 2D along-flow numerical experiments (Gagliardini et al. 2013). We depth-averaged the results of these numerical experiments to first validate our analytical model in §3.1. We then extend the analysis to investigate the stress and velocity perturbation structures for cases with nonlinear sliding law (e.g., 𝑚≠1) and rheologies (e.g., Glen’s flow law, 𝑛= 3) as depth-averaged quantities and at the ice surface in §3.2, generalizing the above-derived scaling relationships to these cases. 3.1. Comparison between numerical experiments and analytical model (𝑚= 1 𝑛= 1) For this set of experiments, we used the same flow parameters as in the analytical model, namely Newtonian ice (𝑛= 1, 𝜂= 1014 Pa s) and basal boundary condition outside of the slippery patch characterized by no-slip (𝛾= 3/2) or by some amount of sliding where 𝛾= [0.1, 1.0], with ranges in surface slope, 𝛼= [0.1◦, 0.25◦, 0.5◦, 1.0◦], thickness, ℎ= [300 m, 1000 m, 2000 m, 3000 m], and patch length, ℓ= [2 km, 5 km, 10 km, 20 km]. These simulations yield stress perturbations for a range of 𝛾, 𝛼, ℎ, ℓthat collapse well in the non-dimensional coordinates ˜𝑥, ˜𝑥∗, ˜𝜏𝑥𝑥(figure 2a,b), showing good agreement with the analytical model (equation 2.14, green curves in figure 2b). All symbols in all panels are denoted by the same legend in panel a. The scaling relationships as revealed by the analytical model (equation 2.14) hold for the numerical results, where the stress magnitude scales linearly with slope and patch length (figure 2f), and the spatial extent of the stress perturbation scales (the “coupling lengthscale” 𝐶𝐿) linearly with the ice thickness divided by the square root of the sliding ratio, 𝛾(equation 2.4, figure 2c). In order to generate this coupling length (𝐶𝐿) comparison between the numerical experiments and the analytical scaling, we define the numerical coupling length (𝐶𝐿) to be distance between the location of maximum stress, max(𝜏𝑥𝑥) (near the transition point at the upstream patch edge), and the upstream location at which the stress decays to below 1/𝑒of the maximum value (figure 2c). This reflects the exponential nature of the stress decay in the analytical model. The numerical coupling lengths (𝐶𝐿) show good agreement with the theoretical coupling lengths (ℎ/√𝛾) (figure 2c), and can be expressed as 𝐶𝐿= 𝜅2ℎ/√𝛾where 𝜅2 = 1.380+0.19 −0.17 is a numerical prefactor fitted across a range of 𝛾, 𝛼, ℎ, ℓfor which 𝜆> 2. We restrict ourselves to this limit of 𝜆as we expect our analysis to break down in the limit as the length of the patch approaches the ice thickness (see Appendix B). The maximum 𝜏𝑥𝑥in the simulation is slightly less than that in the analytical model, 𝜌𝑔ℓ𝛼/4 (equation 2.14, figure 2b,d). This is expected, as the analytical model follows from the SSA, which does not capture higher-order dynamics within the boundary layers between the patch and the far-field flow. That said, we observed that the numerical maximum extensional stress (vertical axis in figure 2d) follows the same scaling as the analytical model and can be expressed as max(𝜏𝑥𝑥) = 𝜅1𝜌𝑔ℓ𝛼/4, where 𝜅1 ≈0.931+0.09 −0.09 is a numerical prefactor fitted across a range of 𝛾, 𝛼, ℎ, ℓfor which 𝜆> 2 (figure 2e). Thus, while the analytical depth- averaged stress expression (equation 2.14) slightly overestimates the maximum stress, this overestimation is consistent across all experiments and captured by the constant numerical prefactor, 𝜅1. The analytical model (equation 2.14) thus sufficiently provides the scaling, i.e. the parameter dependence, of the (1) maximum stress (≈𝜌𝑔ℓ𝛼/4) and the (2) coupling length scale (≈ℎ/√𝛾) with good predictive power across a range of 𝛾, 𝛼, ℎ, ℓ. Similarly, the velocity perturbations extracted from these same numerical experiments collapse in the dimensionless coordinates (figure 3a,b) and agree well with the analytical results (equation 2.18, green curves in figure 3b) across the range of 𝛾, 𝛼, ℓ, ℎ. The maximum perturbed velocity exhibits excellent agreement with the analytical prediction (figure 3c), 9 Figure 2: Depth-averaged numerical results for a selection of the numerical experiments with Newtonian ice and linear sliding law outside the patch (e.g., 𝑛= 1, 𝑚= 1 𝑛) for direct comparison to analytical model, showing (a) the perturbed stress across the domain, (b) non-dimensionalized stresses across the domain in the upstream, patch, and downstream regions, compared to the analytical model (green), and scaling relationships and regression fits (green) for (c) coupling length and (d-f) stress. The solid green lines in (c-f) are the best fits, with the shaded areas representing the ±1𝜎envelope of the data scatter. providing a scaling relationship showing the parameter dependence of the maximum velocity as max(𝑢𝑒𝑙𝑚𝑒𝑟) = 𝜅3(𝐴𝜌𝑔𝛼ℓℎ/4)( √︁ 2/𝛾+ 𝜆/4) + 𝑢𝐿, with a numerical prefactor 𝜅3 = 0.969+0.05 −0.05. The decay of stress outside the patch is exponential (equation 2.14), and therefore so is the velocity decay (equation 2.18), giving decay lengthscale (≈ℎ/√𝛾), as expected. The analytical model best describes cases that are subjected to some amount of slip outside the patch region (e.g., 𝛾< 3/2; no-slip corresponds to 𝛾= 3/2), which is to be expected due to the model’s basis in SSA. To fully capture the dynamics in the transition zone adjacent to the slippery patch (especially for the no-slip to free-slip cases, solid curves, figures 2, 3) requires a full-Stokes analysis in two dimensions (e.g., Barcilon & MacAyeal 1993; Chugunov & Wilchinsky 1996; Mantelli et al. 2019). In utilizing the SSA to model the problem at hand, our aim is to identify the simplest model that reveals the dominant structure of stress and velocity perturbations in response to a slippery patch. The full boundary layer analysis is left for future work. 3.2. Comparison between numerical experiments and scaling arguments (general 𝑛, 𝑚) Here, we extend the simulations to include a broader range of cases. Namely, we use a sliding law characterized by an exponent deviating from the Weertman-type exponent, relaxing the requirement of 𝑚= 1 𝑛, and present the stress and velocity structure across the surface, in addition to depth-averaged. The scaling relationships from the theory are extended as well. Namely, the scale for the stress magnitude remains the same (≈𝜌𝑔𝛼ℓ/4), but the scale for the coupling length (equation 3.2) and maximum velocity (equation 3.3) are now dependent upon the sliding law exponent, 𝑚, and the stress exponent, 𝑛. We reach this conclusion through the following scaling argument. Let’s denote the region of interest, which sits upstream from the onset of the slippery patch, as the boundary layer, where the velocity and horizontal extent occupied by this boundary layer are denoted as [𝑢𝑏𝑙] and [𝑥𝑏𝑙], respectively. The upstream ice in this boundary layer, which is subjected to extensional stress, 10 Figure 3: Depth-averaged numerical results for a selection of the numerical experiments with Newtonian ice and linear sliding law outside the patch (e.g., 𝑛= 1, 𝑚= 1 𝑛) for direct comparison to analytical model, showing (a) the perturbed velocity across the domain, (b) non-dimensionalized velocity across the domain in the upstream, patch, and downstream regions, compared to the analytical model (green), and (c) scaling relationship and regression fit (green) for the maximum velocity magnitude. The solid green line in (c) is the best fit, with the shaded area representing the ±1𝜎envelope of the data scatter. [𝜏𝑥𝑥] = 𝜌𝑔𝛼ℓ/4, set by the presence of the patch, is characterized by the sliding law with 𝐶≡𝛾𝑚(𝜌𝑔𝛼)(1−𝑚𝑛) 𝐴−𝑚ℎ(1−𝑚−𝑚𝑛) (equation 2.5). Combining the assumption of a power- law rheology, [𝑢𝑏𝑙]/[𝑥𝑏𝑙] = 𝐴[𝜏𝑥𝑥]𝑛, with a balance between extensional stress and basal shear in the transition r